[THOUGHT] All eight evidence domains have now been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset → occasional knee pain (no acute trauma);  
2) Course → intermittent, non-progressive, no clear duration pattern;  
3) Associated symptoms → no swelling, locking, giving way, morning stiffness, back/heel pain, or systemic features;  
4) Past medical history → confirmed diagnosis of knee osteoarthritis;  
5) Medications & allergies → occasional acetaminophen, no allergies;  
6) Social/exposure → unable to specify occupation/activity, but no high-risk exposures reported;  
7) Targeted physical exam → bony enlargement requested (key OA sign);  
8) Imaging → weight-bearing radiographs requested (gold standard for structural OA confirmation).  

No red flags for infection, inflammation, trauma, or systemic disease are present. The clinical picture is fully consistent with chronic, symptomatic knee osteoarthritis — the most common cause of intermittent, activity-related knee pain in adults with known OA. All alternative diagnoses (e.g., meniscal tear, gout, RA, septic joint) are strongly disfavored by the absence of supporting features. With 16 turns exhausted and all domains covered, diagnostic synthesis is complete.

[DIAGNOSIS] Knee osteoarthritis

